<td id="S4.T1.4.1.16.13" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">-</td>
<td id="S4.T1.4.1.16.14" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Detection Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents a comparison of MOLM with representative watermarking baselines on Stable Diffusion v1.5. We group prior methods into two categories: <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bit-Recovery methods</span>, which embed explicit binary keys and are evaluated by bit accuracy namely Stable Signature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fernandez et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, AquaLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and WOUAF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; and <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detection-Only methods</span>, which provide binary watermark presence/absence decisions and are evaluated by TPR at <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\%</annotation></semantics></math> FPR namely Tree-Ring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, ROBIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and Gaussian-Shading <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.
MOLM achieves strong detection performance with bit accuracy above <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.98</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.98</annotation></semantics></math> on clean images and robust recovery across all tested distortions. Details on included distortions are in (Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E More Visual Examples ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>). All results in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are averaged over each two variations of these distortions. MOLM retains <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.91" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.91</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.91</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy under cropping and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.89" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.89</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.89</annotation></semantics></math> under JPEG compression. In contrast, Stable Signature suffers significant drops under rotation (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.56" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.56</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.56</annotation></semantics></math>) and resizing (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.72</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.72</annotation></semantics></math>), while AquaLoRA exhibits poor rotation robustness (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.45</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.45</annotation></semantics></math>). WOUAF performs competitively under some distortions but at higher training cost (Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ B.2 Efficiency ‣ Appendix B Setup ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). These results highlight the advantage of MOLM’s routing-based embedding in maintaining consistent key recovery across perturbations. MOLM also compares favorably to detection-only baselines. Tree-Ring and Gaussian-Shading achieve strong TPR under clean or mild distortions, but their detection remains limited to binary watermark presence without key recovery. Moreover, both require full inversion during verification. ROBIN introduces adversarial optimization but struggles under rotation (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.64</annotation></semantics></math>). By contrast, MOLM achieves high TPR (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.95</annotation></semantics></math>) while simultaneously enabling explicit key recovery. On FLUX, MOLM maintains consistent detection accuracy (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{TPR}\geq 0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>TPR</mtext><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{TPR}\geq 0.96</annotation></semantics></math>), confirming that it generalizes beyond Stable Diffusion. Similarly, testing on LAION Aesthetics with models trained on MS-COCO shows that MOLM’s watermark remains recoverable even under distribution shifts. This suggests that MOLM is not tied to a single dataset or architecture, but instead provides a transferable mechanism for embedding and detecting watermarks in generative models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Efficiency ‣ Appendix B Setup ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> we highlight computational efficiency. Unlike Stable Signature, which requires heavy pre-training and per-key training, and AquaLoRA/WOUAF, which demands days of GPU compute, MOLM trains within <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>1 day on a single A100 with no per-key retraining. At inference time, MOLM introduces no overhead beyond the frozen generator, ensuring practical deployability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Image Generation Quality</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next evaluate whether MOLM preserves the perceptual quality of generated images. Quantitatively, we achieve FID values comparable to or lower than existing watermarking methods (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), indicating minimal degradation. Importantly, the differences relative to vanilla Stable Diffusion are small (<math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 1.5</annotation></semantics></math> FID), with no systematic drop in SSIM or PSNR. Qualitative comparisons are shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.5 Image Generation Quality ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. On both MS COCO and LAION Aesthetics, MOLM outputs remain visually indistinguishable from baseline Stable Diffusion generations. The routing mechanism does not introduce noticeable artifacts. Additional visual examples are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E More Visual Examples ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S4.F3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:105pt;vertical-align:-50.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-27.3pt,7.2pt) scale(0.879370275565373,0.879370275565373) ;">
