<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Watermark Robustness</h3>

<section id="S4.SS6.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.6.1 </span>Compression and Diffusion-based Removal Attacks</h4>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> shows that invisible image watermarks can be removed via compression and diffusion-based regeneration attacks. We evaluate three families of attacks: two learned compression models (bmshj2018 and cheng2020) and a diffusion/noise attacker, and report both bit accuracy and FID of the attacked images in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.6.1 Compression and Diffusion-based Removal Attacks ‣ 4.6 Watermark Robustness ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Results are shown for an extractor trained <em id="S4.SS6.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without</em> augmentation (“no-aug”) and <em id="S4.SS6.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">with</em> augmentation (“aug-trained”). Augmentation training significantly enhances robustness to compression and diffusion removal attacks, maintaining high bit accuracy with only minor increases in FID.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness under compression, diffusion, and adversarial removal attacks.</span>
We report Bit Accuracy (↑) and FID (↓). <math id="S4.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the compression quality (lower = stronger),
“steps” denotes denoising iterations, and <math id="S4.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is the MSE bound for PGD attacks.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:53.8pt;vertical-align:-25.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-73.1pt,9.9pt) scale(0.731122461609552,0.731122461609552) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.4.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Params</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.4.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No-Aug</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.4.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aug-Trained</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.4.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row"></th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">Bit Acc.</th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">FID</th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">Bit Acc.</th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">FID</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.4.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">BMSHJ2018</th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1,4,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1,4,8</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.50 / 0.77 / 0.96</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">29.9 / 28.2 / 27.8</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.61 / 0.95 / 0.99</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">30.6 / 28.6 / 28.4</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.4.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Cheng2020</th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1,3,6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1,3,6</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.70 / 0.82 / 0.93</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">29.2 / 27.7 / 27.6</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.94 / 0.95 / 0.97</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">30.1 / 28.9 / 28.7</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.4.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Diffusion Regen.</th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">steps=30,60,100</th>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.72 / 0.69 / 0.62</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">27.8 / 28.4 / 28.5</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.85 / 0.85 / 0.82</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">30.2 / 29.9 / 31.2</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.4.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Adversarial (PGD)</th>
<th id="S4.T2.4.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=10^{-3},10^{-2},10^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=10^{-3},10^{-2},10^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.93 / 0.81 / 0.60</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">27.7 / 27.8 / 28.4</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1.00 / 0.99 / 0.96</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">28.4 / 28.6 / 29.0</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.6.2 </span>Averaging Attacks</h4>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Yang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> recently proposed a simple but effective steganalysis attack against several modern watermarks. Their key observation is that many watermarking schemes across multiple generations maintain a consistent watermark while the image content varies. By averaging <math id="S4.SS6.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> generated images, an adversary can estimate and subtract the watermark for removal, or induce watermark signals into clean images for forgery. We follow their evaluation protocol by generating sets of <math id="S4.SS6.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{5,10,20,50,100,200,500,1000,2000,5000\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>500</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2000</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{5,10,20,50,100,200,500,1000,2000,5000\}</annotation></semantics></math> images and computing averages in both grey-box and black-box settings. We then test two attack variants: Removal and Forgery. We also consider a “same-message” setting where all averaged images embed the identical key. The Figure in the Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Averaging Attacks ‣ Appendix C Robustness ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a> compares MOLM with WOUAF under averaging attacks. Under forgery attempts, MOLM remains at chance accuracy (<math id="S4.SS6.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>) even at higher <math id="S4.SS6.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values, even in the same-message case. Under removal attempts, MOLM maintains high bit accuracy (<math id="S4.SS6.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.96</annotation></semantics></math>) even when averaging up to <math id="S4.SS6.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5000</annotation></semantics></math> images, while WOUAF degrades, with accuracy falling below <math id="S4.SS6.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.90</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.90</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.6.3 </span>Adversarial Attacks on Key Extraction (White-box)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate robustness against adaptive adversaries, we study white-box attacks that directly optimize the input image against the watermark extractor. The adversary has full access to the extractor parameters and detection threshold <math id="S4.SS6.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, and may perturb an image subject to a perceptual constraint. In our experiments, this constraint is expressed as an upper bound on the mean squared error (MSE) with respect to a reference image; equivalently, it can be stated as a lower bound on PSNR. Details on this attack can be found in the Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Adversarial Attacks on Key Extraction (White-box) ‣ Appendix C Robustness ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.6.1 Compression and Diffusion-based Removal Attacks ‣ 4.6 Watermark Robustness ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, adversarial PGD attacks under an MSE constraint <math id="S4.SS6.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> significantly reduce key accuracy when no augmentation is used during training (dropping from 0.93 to 0.60 as <math id="S4.SS6.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> increases). With augmentation, however, MOLM remains robust, achieving <math id="S4.SS6.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;0.96</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy even for <math id="S4.SS6.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> values corresponding to PSNR <math id="S4.SS6.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 10</annotation></semantics></math>. Constraints looser than this threshold lead to visible distortions, indicating that successful removal requires perceptual degradation. We also explore a full-knowledge adversary who retrains the generative model independently; results in the Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Full Knowledge Attack Scenario ‣ Appendix C Robustness ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a> show that such attacker-generated images yield bit accuracy <math id="S4.SS6.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., indistinguishable from random guessing.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.7 </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

<div id="S4.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key Capacity.</span>
The capacity of MOLM is determined by the number of blocks <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> selected from the backbone <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of available adapter paths <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> per block. Since choosing among <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> adapters requires <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math> bits, the total key size is <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=L\cdot\log_{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=L\cdot\log_{2}P</annotation></semantics></math>. In our default configuration, we route the whole <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=14</annotation></semantics></math> ResNet blocks in the VAE decoder with <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=4</annotation></semantics></math> adapters each, yielding <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=14\times 2=28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>14</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=14\times 2=28</annotation></semantics></math> bits per image. Each ResNet block is treated as a single routing layer, where one index <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> determines the active convolutional adapters within the block. We also investigate several extensions:
(i) Independent bits per convolution, which doubles capacity by assigning separate bits to the two convolutional layers inside each ResNet block; (ii) Including attention blocks, where routing cross- and self-attention layers increases <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and thus the total key size;
(iii) Expanding adapter paths, e.g., increasing <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> from 4 to 8 so that each block encodes <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}8=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}8=3</annotation></semantics></math> bits. Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Key Capacity ‣ Appendix D Ablations ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> reports the empirical trade-offs between capacity, accuracy, and fidelity under these configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mapping Effect.</span>
To analyze how key bits are distributed across routing adapters, we conduct a block-wise perturbation probe on the VAE decoder (details in the Appendix <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 Mapping Effect ‣ Appendix D Ablations ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>). Averaging results over 100 random prompts reveals that the mapping is largely distributed: many routing blocks influence multiple bits with intermediate probability, and individual bits are affected by several blocks. While certain bits are more sensitive, there is no strict one-to-one correspondence between adapters and bits. Instead, the key is redundantly encoded across blocks, which enhances robustness, as perturbing any single adapter does not deterministically erase the watermark.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sampling Configurations.</span>
We evaluate watermark robustness under variations in generation settings, including scheduler type, number of denoising steps, and CFG scale. Results in the Appendix <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4 Sampling Configurations ‣ Appendix D Ablations ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a> show consistently high bit accuracy (<math id="S4.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.94</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.94</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.96</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.96</annotation></semantics></math>) across all configurations, with only modest variation in FID (25.8–28.5). This demonstrates that MOLM does not depend on a fixed sampler, unlike inversion-based methods such as Tree-Ring, Gaussian-Shading, or Robin, which require deterministic sampling for detection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adapter Rank.</span>
Finally, we investigate the effect of LoRA adapter rank on watermark fidelity and accuracy. Table in the Appendix <a href="#A4.SS4.SSS1" title="D.4.1 Adapter Rank ‣ D.4 Sampling Configurations ‣ Appendix D Ablations ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4.1</span></a> shows that higher ranks yield stronger bit recovery but slightly increase FID, while very low ranks (8) fail to sustain reliable decoding. This highlights a trade-off between efficiency and watermark robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced a general framework that views watermarking as key-dependent perturbations of frozen generative models, and proposed MOLM as a routing-based instantiation. MOLM trains only lightweight adapters, requires no pretraining on watermarked data, and scales capacity through routing choices. Looking forward, our framework suggests several directions for future work. Exploring alternative routing mechanisms, extending beyond image generation to multimodal models, and analyzing theoretical limits on watermark capacity could further advance the practicality and reliability of watermarking in generative models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reproducibility Statement.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All datasets and models used in this paper are publicly available. We provide detailed descriptions of model architectures, routing configurations, training objectives, and hyperparameters in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Proposed Method ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Section <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Experimental Setup ‣ Appendix B Setup ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, with additional ablation studies in the appendix. Descriptions of the robustness evaluation protocols (distortions, regeneration, averaging, and adversarial PGD) are included in Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6 Watermark Robustness ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>. All comparison methods were implemented using their publicly available codebases
on Stable Diffusion v1.5 to ensure fairness and reproducibility.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
