</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Robustness</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Averaging Attacks</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ C.1 Averaging Attacks ‣ Appendix C Robustness ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> provides the comparison between MOLM and WOUAF under averaging attacks in the same-message setting.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.00293v1/molm_vs_wouaf_averaging.svg" id="A3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/193;" width="548" height="193"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Averaging attack evaluation: MOLM vs. WOUAF (same message). (Left) Forgery attack. MOLM stays at the chance level (<math id="A3.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>). (Right) Removal attack. MOLM achieves accuracy <math id="A3.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.96</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas WOUAF degrades to <math id="A3.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 0.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0.85</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 0.85</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A3.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.90</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.90</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Adversarial Attacks on Key Extraction (White-box)</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we formalize the PGD-based adversarial attack. The constraint is an upper bound on
the mean squared error (MSE) w.r.t. a reference image; equivalently, a lower bound on PSNR.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, given a reference watermarked image <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> and an MSE bound
<math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, the adversarial image <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy</p>
<table id="A3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MSE}(\mathbf{x},\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}})\;\leq\;\varepsilon,\qquad\mathrm{MSE}(\mathbf{x},\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}})\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\,\left\|\mathbf{x}-\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}}\right\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>MSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>MSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MSE}(\mathbf{x},\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}})\;\leq\;\varepsilon,\qquad\mathrm{MSE}(\mathbf{x},\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}})\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\,\left\|\mathbf{x}-\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\mathrm{ref}}\right\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=3HW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=3HW</annotation></semantics></math> (RGB images). Equivalently, a PSNR lower bound <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PSNR}_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PSNR</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PSNR}_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to</p>
<table id="A3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varepsilon_{\star}\;=\;10^{-\mathrm{PSNR}_{\star}/10}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn> 10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>PSNR</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{\star}\;=\;10^{-\mathrm{PSNR}_{\star}/10},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">since for images in <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PSNR}=10\log_{10}(1/\mathrm{MSE})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PSNR</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>MSE</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PSNR}=10\log_{10}(1/\mathrm{MSE})</annotation></semantics></math>. We implement
attacks via <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> projected gradient descent (PGD).</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Extractor notation.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\eta}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{u}=[u_{1},\dots,u_{M}]\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\eta}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{u}=[u_{1},\dots,u_{M}]\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the extractor logits,
and define per-bit probabilities <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}(\mathbf{x})=\sigma(u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}(\mathbf{x})=\sigma(u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a watermarked image <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> with embedded key <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}\in\{0,1\}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}\in\{0,1\}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the
adversary aims to make <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> uninformative (i.e., outputs close to random guessing).
We optimize the removal loss</p>
<table id="A3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rem}}(\mathbf{x})\;=\;\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M}\Big(p_{i}(\mathbf{x})-\tfrac{1}{2}\Big)^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>rem</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rem}}(\mathbf{x})\;=\;\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M}\Big(p_{i}(\mathbf{x})-\tfrac{1}{2}\Big)^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{k}}(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{round}\big(\sigma(V_{\eta}(\mathbf{x}))\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>round</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{k}}(\mathbf{x})=\mathrm{round}\big(\sigma(V_{\eta}(\mathbf{x}))\big)</annotation></semantics></math> be the decoded key.
Removal is considered successful if after <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> PGD iterations the detection statistic
<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\!\left(\mathbf{k},\,\hat{\mathbf{k}}(\mathbf{x}^{(T)})\right)&lt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.686em;" width="0.686em"><mi>D</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\!\left(\mathbf{k},\,\hat{\mathbf{k}}(\mathbf{x}^{(T)})\right)&lt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the verifier fails to
recognize the watermark.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Full Knowledge Attack Scenario</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a white-box adversary who knows the entire {MOLM pipeline (architecture, losses, and training hyperparameters) and is allowed to retrain the generative model on a different subset of the same training data, using different random seeds. The attacker trains their own version and then evaluates the attacker-generated images using our watermark extractor. Across our experiments, our extractor recovers bits from attacker images with average bit-accuracy <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., no better than random guessing. We attribute this failure because routing adapters and the extractor are trained end-to-end, the extractor learns to decode the specific routing-induced activation patterns produced by that training run. Small changes in seed, data order, or subset yield different routing statistics, so an independently trained attacker produces images whose routing signature the defender’s extractor cannot decode.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Ablations</h2>

<figure id="A4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="A4.F5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A4.F5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.F5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Stable Diffusion</th>
<th id="A4.F5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">Decoder-only MOLM (28-bit)</th>
<th id="A4.F5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">UNet-routed MOLM (108-bit)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A4.F5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.F5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.00293v1/images/sd_2.png" id="A4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:137/137;" width="137" height="137" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="A4.F5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.00293v1/figures/unet_decoder.png" id="A4.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:137/137;" width="137" height="137" alt="Refer to caption"></td>
<td id="A4.F5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.00293v1/figures/unet_full.png" id="A4.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:137/137;" width="137" height="137" alt="Refer to caption"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="A4.F5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Routing the UNet.</span> Comparison between vanilla Stable Diffusion (left), MOLM with decoder-only routing (28 bits, middle), and MOLM with UNet routing (108 bits, right). While capacity increases, routing the UNet introduces visible artifacts and degrades fidelity.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Routing the UNet</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to routing the VAE decoder, we experimented with extending routing to the UNet. The UNet in Stable Diffusion v1.5 consists of 22 ResNet blocks, 16 cross-attention layers, and 16 self-attention layers. Treating each of these modules as routing layers, with <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=4</annotation></semantics></math> adapters per layer (2 bits each), results in a total of 108 key bits per image. The image quality degraded significantly compared to decoder-only routing. FID increased by <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.1</annotation></semantics></math>5 points, and human inspection revealed a change in the content compared to clean images as shown in Figure <a href="#A4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix D Ablations ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, especially around fine textures and edges. This suggests that the heavier intervention in the UNet perturbs denoising dynamics more strongly than in the decoder, amplifying the perceptual cost.
These results indicate that while routing the UNet offers higher key capacity, it compromises fidelity. This trade-off highlights the importance of carefully balancing watermark capacity against image quality. We leave a full exploration of UNet routing — potentially with selective layer choice or regularization, as future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Key Capacity</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ D.2 Key Capacity ‣ Appendix D Ablations ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports the quantitative trade-offs between key size, bit accuracy, detection performance (TPR@1%FPR), and image quality (FID) under different architectural configurations. Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ D.2 Key Capacity ‣ Appendix D Ablations ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows visual examples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="A4.T4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key capacity scaling in MOLM.</span>
We report the effective key size (bits), Bit Accuracy (↑), TPR@1%FPR (↑), and FID (↓) for different architectural configurations.</figcaption>
<table id="A4.T4.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
