<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Mapping Effect</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we provide the detailed block-wise perturbation procedure used to generate the flip heatmaps shown in Figure <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ D.3 Mapping Effect ‣ Appendix D Ablations ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Starting from a decoded watermarked image <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we iterate over routing blocks. For each block <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, we identify the active adapter path from the routing mask, temporarily replace its weights with random values of the same shape, re-decode the image using the unchanged routing mask, and run the trained extractor to obtain a predicted key. The original weights are then restored before moving to the next block. Let <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}\in\{0,1\}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}\in\{0,1\}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the embedded key and <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{k}}^{(b)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{k}}^{(b)}</annotation></semantics></math> the extractor output after perturbing block <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the binary flip matrix</p>
<table id="A4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{b,j}\;=\;\mathbbm{1}\{\tilde{k}^{(b)}_{j}\neq k_{j}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_double-struck" mathvariant="double-struck"> 1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{b,j}\;=\;\mathbbm{1}\{\tilde{k}^{(b)}_{j}\neq k_{j}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{b,j}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{b,j}=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that bit <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> flipped when block <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> was randomized.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.00293v1/figures/flipping.png" id="A4.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/241;" width="548" height="241" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Key 1</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.00293v1/figures/flipping2.png" id="A4.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/241;" width="548" height="241" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Key 2</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Bit-flip heatmaps for two different embedded keys, each averaged over 100 random prompts. Rows correspond to routing blocks and columns to key bits; brighter cells indicate a higher probability that the bit flips when the corresponding block is perturbed.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Sampling Configurations</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ D.4 Sampling Configurations ‣ Appendix D Ablations ‣ MOLM: Mixture of LoRA Markers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports the results.
Across all tested configurations, MOLM consistently achieves high bit accuracy (<math id="A4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.94</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.94</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.96</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.96</annotation></semantics></math>), demonstrating robustness to changes in the sampling procedure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T6" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span><span id="A4.T6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sampling and ablation studies.</span>
<span id="A4.T6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Effect of sampling configurations on MOLM. Bit accuracy remains consistently high (<math id="A4.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.94</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.94</annotation></semantics></math>), while FID varies modestly across settings.
<span id="A4.T6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Ablation on the rank of LoRA adapters. Higher ranks improve bit recovery but slightly increase FID.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="A4.T6.8" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:190.8pt;">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A4.T6.8.1" class="ltx_tr">
